WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.792
With all the excitement around ChatGPT, it's easy to lose sight of the unique risks of generative AI.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:13.890
Large language models, a form of generative AI, are really good at helping people who struggle with writing English prose.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.163
It can help them unlock the written word at low cost and sound like a native speaker.

00:00:19.163 --> 00:00:24.338
But because they're so good at generating the next syntactically correct word,

00:00:24.338 --> 00:00:31.049
large language models may give a false impression that they possess actual understanding or meaning.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:40.829
The results can include a flagrantly false narrative directly as a result of its calculated predictions versus a true understanding.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.659
So ask yourself: What is the cost of using an AI that could spread misinformation?

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:50.160
What is the cost to your brand, your business, individuals or society?

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.520
Could your large language model be hijacked by a bad actor?

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.259
Let me explain how you can reduce your risk.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:02.909
It falls into four areas: Hallucinations, Bias, Consent, and Security.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.340
As I present each risk, I'll also call out the strategies you can use to mitigate these risks.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.177
You ready?

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.980
Let's start with the falsehoods, often referred to as "AI hallucinations".

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:22.099
Quick sidebar -- I really don't like the word "hallucinations" because I fear it anthropomorphizes AI.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.664
I'll explain it a bit.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.588
Okay, you've probably heard the news reports of large language models claiming they're human,

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:33.580
or claiming they have emotions, or just stating things that are factually wrong.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.108
What's actually going on here?

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.921
Well, large language models predict the next best syntactically correct word,

00:01:40.921 --> 00:01:46.569
not accurate answers based on understanding of what the human is actually asking for.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:52.090
Which means it's going to sound great, but might be 100% wrong in its answer.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.450
This wrong answer is a statistical error.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.519
Let's take a simple example.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:02.590
Who authored the poems A, B, C?

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:12.190
Let's say they were all authored by the poet X, but there's one source claiming it was the author Z.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.159
We have conflicting sources in the training data.

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.679
Which one actually wins the argument?

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.990
Even worse, there may not be a disagreement at all, but again, a statistical error.

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:27.429
The response could very well be incorrect because again,

00:02:27.429 --> 00:02:35.110
the large language models do not understand meaning; these inaccuracies can be exceptionally dangerous.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:43.150
It's even more dangerous when you have large language models annotate its sources for totally bogus answers.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.210
Why?

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:50.894
Because it gives the perception it has proof when it just doesn't have any.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:59.509
Imagine a call center that has replaced its personnel with a large language model, and it offers a factually wrong answer to a customer.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.930
Right, here's your factually wrong answer.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:11.539
Now, imagine how much angrier this customer will be when they can't actually offer a correction via a feedback loop.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:16.879
This brings us to our first mitigation strategy: Explainability.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:25.721
Now, you could offer inline explainability and pair a large language model

00:03:25.721 --> 00:03:31.159
with the system that offered real data and data lineage and provenance via a knowledge graph.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.699
Why did the model say what it just said?

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.919
Where did it pull its data from?

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:38.427
Which sources?

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.120
The large language model could provide variations on the answer that was offered by the knowledge graph.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.846
Next risk: Bias.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:56.689
Do not be surprised if the output for your original query only lists white male Western European poets.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.480
Want a more representative answer?

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:06.169
Your prompt would have to say something like, "Can you please give me a list of poets that include women and non-Western Europeans?"

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.159
Don't expect the large language model to learn from your prompt.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:16.070
This brings us to the second mitigation strategy: Culture and Audits.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:24.370
Okay, culture is what people do when no one is looking.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:33.430
It starts with approaching this entire subject with humility, as there is so much that has to be learned and even, I would say, unlearned.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:42.399
You need teams that are truly diverse and multidisciplinary in nature working on AI because AI is a great mirror into our own biases.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:52.462
Let's take the results of our audits of AI models and make corrections to our own organizational culture when there are disparate outcomes.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.250
Audit pre-model deployment as well as post-model deployment.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.346
Okay, next risk is Consent.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.919
Is the data that you are curating representative?

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.009
Was it gathered with consent?

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.079
Are there copyright issues?

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.389
Right! Here's a little copyright symbol.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:13.839
These are things we can and should ask for.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:18.220
This should be included in an easy to find, understandable fact sheet.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.399
Oftentimes we subjects, we have no idea where the heck the training data came from these large language models.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:29.556
Where we were that gathered from? Did the developers hoover the dark recesses of the Internet?

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:39.344
To mitigate consent-related risk, we need combined efforts of auditing and accountability.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:40.620
Right!

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:48.261
Accountability includes establishing AI governance processes, making sure you are compliant to existing laws and regulations,

00:05:48.261 --> 00:05:53.390
and you're offering ways for people to have their feedback incorporated.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.995
Now on to the final risk, Security.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:01.380
Large language models could be used for all sorts of malicious tasks,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:07.098
including leaking people's private information, helping criminals phish, spam, scam.

00:06:07.134 --> 00:06:16.369
Hackers have gotten AI models to change their original programming, endorsing things like racism, suggesting people do illegal things.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:18.893
It's called jailbreaking.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:23.180
Another attack is an indirect prompt injection.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:29.436
That's when a third party alters a website, adding hidden data to change the AI's behavior.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:30.980
The result?

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:38.449
Automation relying on AI potentially sending out malicious instructions without you even being aware.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:47.750
This brings us to our final mitigation strategy, and the one that actually pulls all of this together, and that is education.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.000
All right, let me give you an example.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:58.879
Training a brand new large language model produces as much carbon as over a 100 roundtrip flights between New York and Beijing.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:00.878
I know, crazy, right?

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:05.960
This means it's important that we know the strengths and weaknesses of this technology.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:10.882
It means educating our own people on principles for the responsible curation of AI,

00:07:10.882 --> 00:07:16.459
the risks, the environmental cost, the safeguard rails, as well as what the opportunities are.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:19.893
Let me give you another example of where education matters.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:28.220
Today, some tech companies are just trusting that large language models training data has not been maliciously tampered with.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:32.149
I can buy a domain myself now and fill it with bogus data.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:39.560
By poisoning the dataset with enough examples, you could influence a large language model's behavior and outputs forever.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:42.519
This tech isn't going anywhere.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:48.149
We need to think about the relationship that we ultimately want to have with AI.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:53.634
If we're going to use it to augment human intelligence, we have to ask ourselves the question:

00:07:53.634 --> 00:07:57.514
What is the experience like of a person who has been augmented?

00:07:57.850 --> 00:07:59.748
Are they indeed empowered?

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:07.419
Help us make education about the subject of data and AI far more accessible and inclusive than it is today.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:16.300
We need more seats at the table for different kinds of people with varying skill sets working on this very, very important topic.

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:19.528
Thank you for your time.

